  "analysis": "The Angle of His, which is the anatomical angle between the esophagus and the stomach, plays a significant role in preventing gastroesophageal reflux. This angle helps maintain the closure of the lower esophageal sphincter when the stomach expands, thus preventing the backflow of gastric contents into the esophagus. The other options listed do not directly relate to the prevention of gastroesophageal reflux. The upper esophageal sphincter is involved in swallowing but not in preventing reflux. Hernia of Hiato (hiatal hernia) can actually worsen reflux symptoms by impairing the function of the lower esophageal sphincter. The Purkinje angle is not relevant to gastroesophageal anatomy, and the pyloric sphincter controls the passage of stomach contents into the duodenum, not reflux prevention.",
